.TH sane\-neatnm1000 5 "@PACKAGEVERSION@" "SANE Scanner Access Now Easy"
.IX sane\-neatnm1000
.SH NAME
sane\-neatnm1000 \- SANE backend for the Neat NM-1000 sheetfed scanner
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sane\-neatnm1000
library implements a SANE (Scanner Access Now Easy) backend that provides
access to the Neat NM-1000 Mobile Scanner, a sheetfed portable CIS scanner
with USB id 0x1f44/0x0001.
.PP
Supported resolutions: gray 150/300/600 dpi and color 150/300 dpi.
Scan length is sheetfed-variable: the backend stops on paper-out plus
lid-dark, so the image height depends on the fed sheet.
.PP
No vendor firmware blob is needed. The init scripts and calibration data
used by the backend install to
.IR @DATADIR@/sane/neatnm1000 .
.SH "DEVICE NAMES"
This backend expects device names of the form:
.PP
.RS
.I special
.RE
.PP
Where
.I special
is the USB device name, e.g.
.IR libusb:001:002 .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The contents of the
.I neatnm1000.conf
file is a list of device names and USB ids that correspond to Neat NM-1000
scanners. Empty lines and lines starting with a hash mark (#) are ignored.
.PP
The scanner is normally autodetected by the
.B "usb vendor_id product_id"
statement already included in
.IR neatnm1000.conf :
.PP
.RS
usb 0x1f44 0x0001
.RE
.SH WARM SCANS
The firmware keeps scan state after a finished scan. The backend detects a
repeated scan on the same plugged device and skips the power-on handshake
and the feed arming block, matching the vendor driver's own back-to-back
behavior. A physically replugged scanner is always treated as cold.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @CONFIGDIR@/neatnm1000.conf
The backend configuration file.
.TP
.I @DATADIR@/sane/neatnm1000/
Init scripts (nm1000-ctrl-*.json) and calibration blobs (nm1000-*-out.bin).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SANE_DEBUG_NEATNM1000
If set, the backend writes a protocol trace to stderr.
.TP
.B NEATNM1000_DATA_DIR
Directory holding the init scripts and calibration blobs. Overrides the
installed data directory (developer runs).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR sane (7),
.BR sane\-usb (5),
.BR scanimage (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Pedro and contributors. Reverse-engineered from the vendor driver's USB
traffic.
